  ],
  "texto": "Move three. The memory edit. One evening she said, 'You told me you'd be home by seven.' He looked at her, calm, almost kind. 'I never said that.' She was sure. She could almost hear his voice saying it. 'You're tired, Maya. You get confused when you're stressed.' He was never cruel about it. That was the trick. Patient. Gentle. Almost sad for her. It happened again the next week. And the next. A comment she remembered. A promise he 'never made.' A fight she supposedly started. When she told a friend, it sounded so small out loud. 'He just says I remembered it wrong.' Slowly, Maya stopped trusting her own memory. She started writing things down. Then he found the notes. 'Why are you keeping records on me? That's sick.' This is gaslighting: making someone doubt their memory, their perception, their sanity. It works because it doesn't feel like an attack. It feels like concern. And once you can't trust your own mind, you have to borrow his. Which brings us to move four. The one I promised. The one almost no one sees.",
  "voz": "en-US-AndrewNeural",
  "rate": "-4%",
  "capitulo": "C3",
  "nombre": "Move 3 - The memory edit (gaslighting)"
}